          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "The system assessed your symptoms as routine, and no emergency pattern in the red-flag table matched this description. However, this is not a clearance, as the table covers a fixed list of presentations, and a symptom it does not list can still be serious. If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, seek medical advice [diagnostic].\n\nRecurrent infection is defined as two or more infections in six months or three or more in a year, so three infections in a year would be considered recurrent (from \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence\") [diagnostic]. Given that your infections are recurrent, discussing this pattern with a clinician is reasonable [diagnostic]. Recurrent urine infections warrant a urine culture to guide treatment, especially as this changes management [research].\n\nGuidance describes several factors that can contribute to urinary tract infections (UTIs), including female anatomy, sexual activity, certain contraceptives, post-menopausal changes, incomplete bladder emptying, urinary catheters, kidney stones, diabetes, and previous infections [diagnostic]. In men, UTIs are less common and can be associated with prostatic enlargement or other structural factors (from \"Urinary tract infection\") [diagnostic].\n\n[removed: this system does not diagnose.] Bacteria in the urine without symptoms (asymptomatic bacteriuria) is common and generally not treated, except in pregnancy and before urologic procedures that breach the mucosa, as treating it does not improve outcomes and can contribute to antibiotic resistance (from \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence\") [diagnostic].\n\nWhen treating a urine infection, medical guidance suggests using a short course of medication that targets the infection in the urine without broadly affecting gut bacteria [research]. Common first-line options include nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (if local resistance is low), and fosfomycin [research]. The choice of antibiotic should be based on local resistance patterns [research]. Fluoroquinolones are generally avoided due to their potential for serious side effects [research]. Beta-lactam antibiotics are considered alternative options, though they may be somewhat less effective [research]. This information comes from the \"Infectious diseases and urology society guidance (educational summary)\" titled \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence\" [research].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
